<section id="exec">
<h2>1. Executive Summary &amp; KPIs</h2>
<p>Across the 27,468 weekly observations, the global mean retail petrol price was <b>USD 2.282/L</b>, diesel <b>USD 2.134/L</b>, and LPG <b>USD 1.711/L</b>. Brent Crude averaged <b>USD 106.69/bbl</b>, swinging from a COVID-era trough of <b>$47.97</b> (Apr 2020) to a ceiling of <b>$130.00</b> first hit in March 2022 and revisited in early 2026. Once retail prices are aggregated to a single weekly global mean, they track Brent almost perfectly (<b>r ≈ 1.00</b>); however, at the country-week level the relationship is far noisier (r ≈ 0.26–0.42), reflecting wide policy heterogeneity.</p>

<div class="grid kpi-grid">
  <div class="kpi accent"><div class="label">Avg Retail Petrol</div><div class="value">$2.282</div><div class="sub">USD per liter, 2020–2026 mean</div></div>
  <div class="kpi accent"><div class="label">Avg Retail Diesel</div><div class="value">$2.134</div><div class="sub">USD per liter, 2020–2026 mean</div></div>
  <div class="kpi warn"><div class="label">Brent Crude Avg</div><div class="value">$106.69</div><div class="sub">USD/bbl · range $47.97 → $130.00</div></div>
  <div class="kpi good"><div class="label">Brent–Petrol Correlation</div><div class="value">1.00 / 0.26</div><div class="sub">Weekly global mean vs. country-level</div></div>
  <div class="kpi bad"><div class="label">Subsidy Dampening</div><div class="value">−79.5%</div><div class="sub">High-subsidy vs. Low-subsidy petrol price gap</div></div>
  <div class="kpi accent"><div class="label">2026 Regional Spread</div><div class="value">$2.87</div><div class="sub">Europe ($4.29) − Middle East ($1.42)</div></div>
  <div class="kpi warn"><div class="label">High-income Tax Avg</div><div class="value">34.7%</div><div class="sub">vs. Middle 21.1% · Low 22.9%</div></div>
  <div class="kpi good"><div class="label">Countries Covered</div><div class="value">84</div><div class="sub">7 regions · 3 income tiers · 4 subsidy tiers</div></div>
</div>
<p class="note">Data note: the source brief described <code>subsidy_level</code> as binary (High/Low). The dataset in fact contains four levels — <b>Very High, High, Medium, Low</b>. All analyses use the four observed categories.</p>
</section>

<section id="global-overview">
<h2>2. Global Fuel Price Overview</h2>
<p>The table below summarises the central tendency, dispersion, and observed range of each fuel as well as Brent Crude over the full 2020–2026 window. Petrol carries the highest mean and the widest spread; LPG remains the cheapest fuel on average. Tax shares vary from effectively 0% in heavily subsidised producers to 65% in highly fiscalised European markets.</p>

<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Variable</th><th class="num">Mean</th><th class="num">Std Dev</th><th class="num">Min</th><th class="num">Max</th><th class="num">Observations</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Petrol (USD/L)</td><td class="num">2.282</td><td class="num">1.541</td><td class="num">0.010</td><td class="num">6.779</td><td class="num">27,468</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Diesel (USD/L)</td><td class="num">2.134</td><td class="num">1.465</td><td class="num">0.010</td><td class="num">6.240</td><td class="num">27,468</td></tr>
