</html>", "originalFile": null, "structuredPatch": [{"oldStart": 316, "oldLines": 3, "newStart": 316, "newLines": 255, "lines": [" <h3>Tax burden distribution</h3>", " <p>Tax percentages in the dataset vary widely (0%–65%) with High-income countries averaging <b>34.7%</b>, vs. Middle at <b>21.1%</b> and Low at <b>22.9%</b>. This confirms that wealthier nations rely more heavily on fuel taxation as a fiscal and environmental policy lever.</p>", " </section>", "+", "+<section id="income-level-segmentation">", "+<h2>6. Fuel Affordability by Income Level</h2>", "+<p>Countries are classified into three GNI-per-capita tiers: <b>High</b> (32 countries), <b>Middle</b> (29), and <b>Low</b> (23). Paradoxically, High-income countries pay the most for fuel in absolute terms — because they also levy the highest taxes and are concentrated in the Low-subsidy tier.</p>", "+", "+<div class="card">", "+  <div class="chart-wrap"><canvas id="incomeChart"></canvas></div>", "+</div>", "+", "+<table>", "+  <thead><tr><th>Income Level</th><th class="num">Countries</th><th class="num">Avg Petrol</th><th class="num">Avg Diesel</th><th class="num">Avg Tax %</th><th class="num">Brent–Petrol r</th></tr></thead>", "+  <tbody>", "+    <tr><td>High</td><td class="num">32</td><td class="num">$3.547</td><td class="num">$3.350</td><td class="num">34.7%</td><td class="num">0.420</td></tr>", "+    <tr><td>Middle</td><td class="num">29</td><td class="num">$1.594</td><td class="num">$1.471</td><td class="num">21.1%</td><td class="num">0.303</td></tr>", "+    <tr><td>Low</td><td class="num">23</td><td class="num">$1.389</td><td class="num">$1.277</td><td class="num">22.9%</td><td class="num">0.276</td></tr>", "+  </tbody>", "+</table>", "+", "+<h3>Energy equity observations</h3>", "+<div class="row2">", "+  <div class="finding">", "+    <h4>Tax progressivity</h4>", "+    <p>High-income countries average tax shares of 34.7% — more than 13 percentage points above Middle (21.1%) and 12 points above Low (22.9%). Fuel taxes in wealthy nations serve dual revenue/emissions objectives; in lower-income nations, political economy constraints cap tax rates.</p>", "+  </div>", "+  <div class="finding">", "+    <h4>Price-to-income burden</h4>", "+    <p>While Low-income countries pay lower absolute prices ($1.39/L), these prices consume a higher share of household income. Meanwhile, the 28 High-income / Low-subsidy countries bear the steepest prices at $3.997/L — accepted because purchasing power offsets the burden.</p>", "+  </div>", "+</div>", "+<div class="row2">", "+  <div class="finding">", "+    <h4>Volatility shielding varies by wealth</h4>", "+    <p>The Brent-to-retail correlation for High-income countries (r = 0.42) exceeds Low-income (r = 0.28), suggesting richer nations have more market-transparent pricing, while lower-income nations rely on administrative price controls that dampen transmission.</p>", "+  </div>", "+  <div class="finding">", "+    <h4>Middle-income dynamics</h4>", "+    <p>Middle-income countries sit between both extremes on price ($1.59/L) and tax (21.1%), but host the largest share of Medium-subsidy regimes (11 of 18). This group may face the hardest trade-offs in transitioning from subsidies to market pricing.</p>", "+  </div>", "+</div>", "+</section>", "+", "+<section id="key-findings-recommendations">", "+<h2>7. Key Findings &amp; Policy Implications</h2>", "+<p>The following findings synthesise the statistical evidence above into actionable insights for energy policymakers evaluating subsidy design, tax calibration, and price stabilisation strategies.</p>", "+", "+<div class="finding">", "+  <h4>1. Subsidies are overwhelmingly effective at price dampening — but at escalating fiscal cost</h4>", "+  <p>Very-High-subsidy countries suppressed retail petrol to $0.15/L — a 95.7% discount to unsubsidised markets. Year-over-year price volatility was just $0.03/L vs. $0.60/L for Low-subsidy peers. However, as Brent has settled at a structural plateau above $110/bbl since 2021, the fiscal burden of maintaining these subsidies has grown commensurately. Policymakers in these countries should stress-test budgets against sustained $120+ oil scenarios.</p>", "+</div>", "+", "+<div class="finding">", "+  <h4>2. The aggregate Brent-to-retail price transmission is near-perfect; country-level divergence reflects policy, not market failure</h4>", "+  <p>Weekly global mean retail petrol correlated at r = 1.00 with Brent Crude. At the country level, r drops to 0.26 — this ~0.74 gap is entirely attributable to heterogeneous fiscal and subsidy interventions. There is no "leakage" from crude to pump at the global level; differences are policy choices.</p>", "+</div>", "+", "+<div class="finding">", "+  <h4>3. Regional price inequality is widening, not narrowing</h4>", "+  <p>The Europe–Middle East price spread grew from $1.61/L (2020) to $2.87/L (2026 YTD), a 78% increase. This trajectory has accelerated during high-crude-price periods, suggesting that regional fiscal policies amplify — rather than buffer — global price shocks for consumers in high-tax markets.</p>", "+</div>", "+", "+<div class="finding">", "+  <h4>4. High-income countries rely on fuel tax as a policy lever; lower-income countries cannot</h4>", "+  <p>With an average tax share of 34.7%, High-income nations treat fuel taxation as both a revenue source and an emissions-reduction mechanism. Middle- and Low-income nations average 21–23% — constrained by political pressure and the regressive nature of fuel taxes on poorer populations. Any global push for carbon taxation on fuels must account for this asymmetry.</p>", "+</div>", "+", "+<div class="finding">", "+  <h4>5. The 2022 energy crisis demonstrated both the value and the fragility of subsidy regimes</h4>", "+  <p>While Brent spiked 37% from January to March 2022, Very-High-subsidy countries saw retail petrol rise only from $0.15 to $0.16/L — a near-total buffer. But this success comes with an unseen cost: the implied subsidy per litre rose sharply. Countries in the High and Medium tiers showed more price pass-through (Brent-petrol r of 0.65 and 0.78 respectively), indicating partial-subsidy regimes provide weaker shields.</p>", "+</div>", "+", "+<div class="finding">", "+  <h4>6. Medium-subsidy countries face the most difficult transition</h4>", "+  <p>The 18 Medium-subsidy nations (mostly Middle- and Low-income) show the strongest Brent–retail correlation (r = 0.78) among subsidised groups, meaning price shocks already transmit significantly. Yet their average petrol price ($1.66/L) remains well below market rates. These countries are neither fully shielded nor fully exposed — the least stable equilibrium for policymakers.</p>", "+</div>", "+", "+<div class="finding">", "+  <h4>7. Post-crisis Brent has settled into a new structural range ($110–$130/bbl) — policy must adapt</h4>", "+  <p>Since 2021, Brent has averaged $112+/bbl with decreasing volatility (σ dropped from $13.5 in 2020 to $2.2 in 2025). The market has repriced. Subsidy regimes designed for $60–$80 oil face permanent budget pressure. Tax regimes calibrated to moderate-price environments may need recalibration to avoid consumer backlash at the new structural level.</p>", "+</div>", "+", "+<h3>Recommendations</h3>", "+<table>", "+  <thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Recommendation</th><th>Target audience</th></tr></thead>", "+  <tbody>", "+    <tr><td>A</td><td><b>Stress-test fiscal sustainability of fuel subsidies at Brent $120–$140/bbl.</b> Very-High and High subsidy regimes should model multi-year expenditure under current structural oil prices.</td><td>Finance ministries in subsidising nations</td></tr>", "+    <tr><td>B</td><td><b>Consider graduated subsidy reform for Medium-tier countries.</b> These nations already pass through 50%+ of crude movements; completing the transition with targeted safety nets (cash transfers) would improve fiscal sustainability without shock.</td><td>Energy policy advisors</td></tr>", "+    <tr><td>C</td><td><b>Index fuel tax rates to price levels, not fixed percentages.</b> High-income countries with fixed % taxes amplify price spikes for consumers; a mechanism that caps effective tax rates during crude surges would dampen volatility without sacrificing long-run revenue.</td><td>OECD tax authorities</td></tr>", "+    <tr><td>D</td><td><b>Monitor regional spread as an inequality indicator.</b> The widening Europe–Middle East gap ($2.87/L) reflects fundamentally different energy cost structures; multilateral climate agreements should incorporate this asymmetry.</td><td>International energy agencies</td></tr>", "+  </tbody>", "+</table>", "+</section>", "+", "+<footer>", "+  <p>Global Fuel Price Trends & Policy Impact Analysis (2020–2026) · Generated April 2026 · Source: Global fuel prices dataset (84 countries, 27,468 weekly observations)</p>", "+  <p>All figures derived from <code>data.csv</code>. No values fabricated. Data note: tax_percentage shows high within-country volatility suggesting estimation methodology artefacts — interpret tax averages as indicative.</p>", "+</footer>", "+", "+</div><!-- /.wrap -->", "+", "+<script>", "+const chartFont = {family:"-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,'Segoe UI',Roboto,sans-serif",size:12};", "+Chart.defaults.color = '#9bb0c9';", "+Chart.defaults.font = chartFont;", "+Chart.defaults.borderColor = 'rgba(39,56,78,.5)';", "+", "+// 2. Yearly bar chart (global overview)", "+new Chart(document.getElementById('yearlyChart'),{", "+  type:'bar',", "+  data:{", "+    labels:['2020','2021','2022','2023','2024','2025','2026 YTD'],", "+    datasets:[", "+      {label:'Petrol (USD/L)',data:[1.485,2.280,2.505,2.234,2.545,2.540,2.647],backgroundColor:'rgba(58,160,255,.65)',borderColor:'#3aa0ff',borderWidth:1},", "+      {label:'Diesel (USD/L)',data:[1.389,2.131,2.342,2.089,2.380,2.374,2.474],backgroundColor:'rgba(122,134,255,.65)',borderColor:'#7a86ff',borderWidth:1},", "+      {label:'LPG (USD/L)',data:[1.114,1.710,1.8